@meta
id: 31-02
title: Where the config lives now
chapter: 31
type: lesson
skills: nvim-differences, lua-config
par: 110
minutes: 5
vscode: settings.json, in a path nobody can recite either
open: answers.txt

@brief
Chapter 28 gave you `~/.vimrc` and `~/.vim/`. Neovim uses neither. It
follows the XDG convention, so your configuration lives in a directory
rather than a file:

    ~/.config/nvim/init.lua        the file Neovim reads at startup
    ~/.config/nvim/lua/            your own modules, loaded with require
    ~/.config/nvim/after/ftplugin/ per-filetype settings, same idea as vim
    ~/.local/share/nvim/           plugins and other data, not config

`init.lua` is the entry point. If you are not ready to write Lua, Neovim
will read `~/.config/nvim/init.vim` instead and run your existing
vimscript almost unchanged — that is the migration path most people take
on day one, and there is nothing wrong with staying on it for a while.
What you must not do is create both: Neovim refuses to guess and stops
with a conflict error instead of picking one.

Inside Neovim, `:echo stdpath('config')` prints the config directory and
`:checkhealth` tells you what is missing. Neither is available here.

Answer the five questions in answers.txt from the paragraphs above. This
lesson is graded in vim, like the whole chapter — you are writing down what
Neovim would do, not running it.

@keys
| `A` | append at the end of the line |
| `j` | down a line — `A` ignores the column, so you can chain them |

@hints
Every answer is on this page. Four of them are paths; the fifth is one
word describing what Neovim does rather than choosing for you.
--
`A` puts you in insert mode at the end of the current line, wherever the
cursor sits on it. `Esc`, `j`, `A` again is the whole rhythm.
--
Start with `/neovim_reads` and Enter, then work down the file with
`A ... Esc j`.

@solution
    /neovim_reads<Enter>
    A~/.config/nvim/init.lua<Esc>
    jA~/.config/nvim<Esc>
    jA~/.config/nvim/init.vim<Esc>
    jAerrors<Esc>
    :wq<Enter>

Ninety-five keystrokes, nearly all of them the answers themselves.

Two details worth carrying:

- The directory, not the file, is what you version control. A Neovim
  config is a small Lua project — `init.lua` plus a `lua/` tree you
  `require` from — and that is why Neovim configs get shared as git repos
  while vimrcs get shared as pastes.
- `init.vim` and `init.lua` are mutually exclusive, but inside `init.lua`
  you can still run any vimscript you have not ported yet with
  `vim.cmd([[ ... ]])`. A migration does not have to be finished to be
  useful.

@start answers.txt
# Neovim configuration paths.
# Put your answer right after the = with no spaces.

vim_reads=~/.vimrc
neovim_reads=
neovim_config_directory=
vimscript_compatibility_file=
if_both_config_files_exist_neovim=

@expected answers.txt
# Neovim configuration paths.
# Put your answer right after the = with no spaces.

vim_reads=~/.vimrc
neovim_reads=~/.config/nvim/init.lua
neovim_config_directory=~/.config/nvim
vimscript_compatibility_file=~/.config/nvim/init.vim
if_both_config_files_exist_neovim=errors
